---
// src/pages/guides/hillsborough-plants.astro
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const businessName = import.meta.env['PUBLIC_BUSINESS_NAME'] || 'Palm & Agave Nursery';
---

<Layout
  title="Plants for Hillsborough County Landscapes — Plant Guides | Palm & Agave Nursery"
  description="Selection guidance for agaves and palms in Hillsborough County, Florida — including Tampa, Brandon, Riverview, Plant City, Valrico, Lutz, and Wesley Chapel. Sun exposure, cold tolerance, and delivery considerations."
>
  <Header cartCount={0} />

  <div class="guide-page">
    <article class="guide-article container">
      <header class="guide-header">
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <ol class="breadcrumb-list">
            <li><a href="/" class="breadcrumb-link">Home</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li><a href="/guides" class="breadcrumb-link">Guides</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li class="breadcrumb-current" aria-current="page">Hillsborough County Plants</li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">Local Guide</p>
        <h1 class="guide-title">Plants for Hillsborough County landscapes</h1>
        <p class="guide-meta">Guidance for selecting agaves and palms across Tampa, Brandon, Riverview, Plant City, Valrico, Lutz, Wesley Chapel, and the surrounding county — with notes on conditions, delivery, and selection.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="guide-content">
        <p class="guide-intro">
          Hillsborough County covers a range of conditions within a relatively compact area — from coastal-influenced sites to inland neighborhoods, from flat wetter spots to better-drained elevated areas. The right plant depends on the site, not just the county label.
        </p>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">About the area</h2>
          <p>
            Hillsborough County is the primary market for this nursery. The county includes Tampa, Brandon, Riverview, Plant City, Valrico, Lutz, Wesley Chapel, Apollo Beach, Ruskin, and many other communities. Conditions vary across the county, and a plant that works well in one part of the county may be a better or worse fit in another.
          </p>
          <p>
            The nursery serves Hillsborough County as its core market. Regional delivery to surrounding counties is available on request, but the primary focus is Hillsborough County itself.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">What to consider when selecting plants</h2>
          <p>
            When choosing agaves and palms for a Hillsborough County landscape, consider these factors first:
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li><strong>Sun exposure</strong> — how much direct sun does the site get, and at what time of day?</li>
            <li><strong>Drainage</strong> — does water stand after rain, or does the site drain well?</li>
            <li><strong>Cold exposure</strong> — is the site open and exposed, or protected by buildings, fences, or canopy?</li>
            <li><strong>Space</strong> — how much room is there now, and how much will there be as plants mature?</li>
            <li><strong>Use</strong> — is this a residential front yard, a commercial entrance, a privacy planting, or a feature specimen?</li>
            <li><strong>Maintenance</strong> — how much care can the owner provide, and what is realistic for the site?</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            These factors matter more than a generic list of "plants that grow in the area." A well-chosen plant in the right spot will outperform a popular plant in the wrong one.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Agaves in Hillsborough County</h2>
          <p>
            Agaves can be excellent choices for Florida landscapes when placed correctly. They are architectural, drought-tolerant once established, and well suited to modern, contemporary, and minimal designs. They also work in container plantings, entrance features, and specimen placements.
          </p>
          <p>
            In Hillsborough County, agaves generally do well in full sun and well-drained soil. The main limitations are drainage, winter cold in exposed sites, and placement near traffic where spiny species could be a problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            Good uses for agaves in the county include:
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>Feature specimens in contemporary or minimal landscapes</li>
            <li>Container plants for patios, entries, and courtyards</li>
            <li>Structure in xeriscape and low-water landscaping</li>
            <li>Commercial landscapes that need a clean, architectural look</li>
            <li>Accent plants in mixed borders where they can be seen and appreciated</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Choose cold-tolerant agaves for exposed locations, and avoid placing spiny agaves where people will brush against them. Compact agaves are useful where space is limited. Specimen agaves work where a single plant needs to make a statement.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Palms in Hillsborough County</h2>
          <p>
            Palms are a natural fit for Florida landscapes, and Hillsborough County supports a wide range of palm species. Cold-hardy palms are the safest choice for most sites, especially exposed ones, while less cold-tolerant palms can work in protected or warmer locations.
          </p>
          <p>
            Palms are useful for:
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>Vertical accent and specimen planting</li>
            <li>Landscape structure and framing</li>
            <li>Shade and screening where the crown is appropriate</li>
            <li>Commercial and residential entrances</li>
            <li>Bulk plantings for larger properties and projects</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Cold-hardy palms such as windmill palms and pindo palms are reliable choices for many sites in the county. Specimen palms such as foxtail palms and Canary Island date palms are suited to larger landscapes and projects where the scale is right.
          </p>
          <p>
            When selecting palms, pay attention to overall height, clear-trunk height, mature size, and growth habit. A palm that looks right at purchase may outgrow a small space quickly if its mature size and growth rate are not considered.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Delivery and pickup</h2>
          <p>
            Delivery within Hillsborough County is available for most orders. Pickup at the nursery is also available and is often the simplest option for smaller orders or for buyers who want to inspect plants before taking them home.
          </p>
          <p>
            For large specimens, field-grown palms, and bulk orders, delivery may require a quote. Oversized plants, heavy root balls, and project quantities can affect transport, unloading, and scheduling. Confirm details before committing to delivery for larger items.
          </p>
          <p>
            If your site has limited access, narrow entry, or requires equipment to unload, mention that when you submit your order or inquiry. Delivery is easier when access issues are discussed in advance.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Common mistakes in this area</h2>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li><strong>Ignoring drainage.</strong> Flat, wet spots can be a problem for agaves and some palms. Improve drainage or choose a better site.</li>
            <li><strong>Underestimating mature size.</strong> A palm that looks fine now can become too large for a space later. Plan for the future.</li>
            <li><strong>Choosing a palm for looks alone.</strong> A palm that looks great in a photo may not suit the site's sun, cold exposure, or clearance needs.</li>
            <li><strong>Placing spiny agaves near traffic.</strong> Sharp agaves near walkways and driveways create hazards.</li>
            <li><strong>Assuming regional delivery is automatic.</strong> Regional delivery is available on request, not as a guaranteed blanket service.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section guide-section--cta">
          <p class="guide-cta-text">
            Ready to choose plants for a Hillsborough County landscape? Browse our agave and palm catalogs, filter by the attributes that matter for your site, and contact us if you need help matching plants to a specific property.
          </p>
          <div class="guide-cta-links">
            <a href="/shop/agaves" class="btn btn-primary">Shop Agaves</a>
            <a href="/shop/palms" class="btn btn-outline">Shop Palm Trees</a>
            <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-outline">Ask a Question</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .guide-page { padding: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-16); }
  .guide-article { max-width: 760px; }

  .breadcrumb { margin-bottom: var(--space-8); padding-bottom: var(--space-5); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .breadcrumb-list { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-2); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  .breadcrumb-link { color: var(--color-text-secondary); text-decoration: none; transition: color var(--transition-fast); }
  .breadcrumb-link:hover, .breadcrumb-link:focus-visible { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .breadcrumb-current { color: var(--color-text-primary); font-weight: var(--font-medium); }

  .guide-eyebrow { font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: var(--font-semibold); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.12em; color: var(--color-sage); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .guide-title { font-size: clamp(var(--text-2xl), 4vw, var(--text-4xl)); color: var(--color-green-deep); line-height: 1.15; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .guide-meta { font-size: var(--text-base); color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: 600px; line-height: 1.6; }

  .guide-content { margin-top: var(--space-10); }
  .guide-intro { font-size: var(--text-lg); line-height: 1.75; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }

  .guide-section { margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }
  .guide-section-heading { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-xl); color: var(--color-green-deep); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .guide-section p { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.75; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }

  .guide-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .guide-list li { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text-secondary); padding-left: var(--space-5); position: relative; }
  .guide-list li::before { content: '—'; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-sage); }

  .guide-section--cta { padding: var(--space-8); background-color: rgba(41, 79, 59, 0.04); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); }
  .guide-cta-text { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .guide-cta-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-3); }
</style>
